@Tutorial(time: 5) {
    @Intro(title: "Going further") {
        You can pat yourself on the back for having completed your first 3D
        game with Xogot.

        In this series, we went over a wide range of techniques and editor
        features. Hopefully, you've witnessed how intuitive Xogot's scene system
        can be and learned a few tricks you can apply in your projects.

        But we just scratched the surface: Xogot has a lot more in store for you
        to save time creating games. And you can learn all that by browsing the
        documentation.

        Where should you begin? Below, you'll find a few pages to start exploring
        and build upon what you've learned so far.

        But before that, here's a link to download a completed version of the
        project: <https://github.com/xogot-projects/Save-the-Axolotl-Snake-Stomp>.
        
        @Image(source: going-01.further-learning-resources.png)
    }
    @Section(title: "Exploring the manual") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The manual is your ally whenever you have a doubt or you're curious
            about a feature. It does not contain tutorials about specific game
            genres or mechanics. Instead, it explains how Xogot works in
            general. In it, you will find information about 2D, 3D, physics,
            rendering and performance, and much more.

            Here are the sections we recommend you to explore next:

            1. Read the
              [Scripting section](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/index.html#toc-scripting-core-features)
              to learn essential programming features you'll use in every
              project.
            2. The
              [3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/3d/index.html#toc-learn-features-3d)
              and
              [Physics](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/physics/index.html#toc-learn-features-physics)
              sections will teach you more about 3D game creation in the engine.
            3.
              [Inputs](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/inputs/index.html#toc-learn-features-inputs)
              is another important one for any game project.

            In this series, we only scratched the surface of working with
            glTF models and animations. To go deeper, read the following
            pages:

            - [Importing 3D scenes](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_3d_scenes.html#doc-importing-3d-scenes)
              covers the import dock, advanced options, and the
              inheritance pattern we used.
            - [Import configuration](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/import_configuration.html#doc-import-configuration)
              explains how to tune per-resource import settings,
              including animation loop modes and compression.
            - [Model export considerations](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/exporting_models/model_export_considerations.html#doc-model-export-considerations)
              gives tips for preparing models in tools like Blender so
              they import cleanly.
            - The [Animation](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/animation/index.html#toc-learn-features-animation)
              section introduces the [AnimationTree](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/animation/animation_tree.html#doc-animation-tree),
              which lets you blend and transition between animations —
              the natural next step beyond the two `AnimationPlayer`s we
              used here.

            You can start with these or, if you prefer, look at the **Sidebar**
            (_Scene Tree_ and _File Tree_) and pick your options.

            We hope you enjoyed this tutorial series, and we're looking forward to
            seeing what you achieve using Xogot.

            @Image(source: going-01.further-learning-resources.png)
        }
        @Steps {
        }
    }
}
